    "body": "You take the left side, neighbour,\nI’ll take the right.\nYou watch the deep dark,\nI’ll watch the light.\n\nMy knife slips out,\nYour knife pulls in.\nI carve the right ear long,\nYou carve the twin.\n\nTwo halves of a hame,\nTwo wheels in the rut.\nYou don't need a map\nWhen the doors are shut.\n\nYou trim the wick at dusk,\nI'll pour the amber slow.\nNever two dark in a row—\nIt's all the creed we know.\n\nJust keep the bench warm, neighbour.\nI’ll drive the grey.\nWe’ll settle the tally\nAt the end of the day.\n\n*(Beneath this, scratched lighter and slower—a different hand, older now:)*\n\nThe bench is still warm. The day is not done. The tally stands. — S."
